"""Quantum statistics, particles, fields and relativistic equations: Bose-Einstein and Fermi-Dirac statistics, boson, fermion, electron, photon, Fock space, QED, QFT, renormalization, Dirac and Klein-Gordon equations, quantum chemistry, Standard Model."""

NARRATIVES = {
    "bose_einstein_statistics": [
        ("p", r"Rubidium atoms cooled in a magnetic trap to a few hundred nanokelvin collect abruptly in the lowest quantum state of the trap. After the trap is switched off, a narrow peak appears in the velocity distribution above a broad thermal cloud. The temperature at which this happens follows from counting how many atoms the excited states can hold. For noninteracting bosons at temperature $T$ and chemical potential $\mu$, Quantum statistical mechanics gives the mean occupation of a single-particle state of energy $\epsilon$ as $\overline n_B=[e^{\beta(\epsilon-\mu)}-1]^{-1}$, with $\beta=1/k_BT$."),
        ("p", r"Occupations cannot be negative, so $\mu$ must stay below the lowest single-particle energy, set here to zero. At a fixed $\mu<0$ every excited state holds a finite mean number of particles. For a uniform gas of spinless particles of mass $m$, the excited states can therefore hold at most the density reached as $\mu\to0$:"),
        ("eq", r"""n_{\rm ex}\leq\int\frac{d^3p}{(2\pi\hbar)^3}\,\frac{1}{e^{\beta p^2/2m}-1}
=\frac{\zeta(3/2)}{\lambda_T^3},\qquad
\lambda_T=\sqrt{\frac{2\pi\hbar^2}{mk_BT}}."""),
        ("p", r"Here $\zeta(3/2)\approx2.612$ is a value of the Riemann zeta function and $\lambda_T$ is the thermal de Broglie wavelength. If the density $n$ exceeds this bound, the surplus cannot be accommodated in excited states. It accumulates in the ground state, whose occupation $(e^{-\beta\mu}-1)^{-1}$ grows without limit as $\mu$ approaches zero from below. The condition $n\lambda_T^3=\zeta(3/2)$ defines the transition temperature, and the same bound gives the condensed fraction below it:"),
        ("eq", r"""k_BT_c=\frac{2\pi\hbar^2}{m}\left(\frac{n}{\zeta(3/2)}\right)^{2/3},\qquad
\frac{N_0}{N}=1-\left(\frac{T}{T_c}\right)^{3/2}."""),
        ("p", r"Condensation thus begins when the thermal wavelength becomes comparable with the spacing $n^{-1/3}$ between particles. No attraction between the atoms is involved. The cause is the counting of symmetric states: a bosonic mode can hold any number of particles, and permuting identical particles produces no new state. With Maxwell--Boltzmann weights the corresponding relation is $n\lambda_T^3=e^{\beta\mu}$, which has no upper bound, so excited states accommodate any density."),
        ("p", r"London applied this result to liquid helium-4 in 1938. A mass density of $0.145\,\mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}}$ gives $n=2.18\times10^{28}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$ and $T_c\approx3.1$ K, close to the observed superfluid transition at 2.17 K. The difference reflects the strong interactions in the liquid, for which ideal-gas counting is only a guide. Neutron scattering finds that less than ten percent of the atoms occupy the zero-momentum state even near absolute zero."),
        ("p", r"Dilute alkali gases approach the ideal limit much more closely. In a harmonic trap with geometric-mean angular frequency $\bar\omega$, the density of states grows as $\epsilon^2$ instead of $\sqrt\epsilon$. The same counting then gives"),
        ("eq", r"""k_BT_c=\hbar\bar\omega\left(\frac{N}{\zeta(3)}\right)^{1/3}
\approx0.94\,\hbar\bar\omega N^{1/3},\qquad
\frac{N_0}{N}=1-\left(\frac{T}{T_c}\right)^3."""),
        ("p", r"For $N=10^6$ atoms and $\bar\omega=2\pi\times100\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ this gives $T_c\approx450$ nK. Condensation was observed in rubidium and sodium in 1995 at transition temperatures between about 0.2 and 2 microkelvin. The results assume many particles and weak interactions. A finite particle number lowers $T_c$ by a relative amount of order $N^{-1/3}$, and repulsive interactions shift it by a few percent in traps. In a uniform two-dimensional gas the corresponding integral diverges as $\mu\to0$, and no condensation occurs at finite temperature."),
        ("p", r"Photons and phonons in thermal equilibrium are not conserved in number. Their chemical potential is therefore zero, and the same occupation gives Planck's law instead of a condensate. Reversing the sign in the denominator gives Fermi--Dirac statistics, which fills states one by one up to a Fermi energy. The symmetric exchange rule itself is discussed under Boson."),
    ],
    "boson": [
        ("p", r"Two photons that reach the two input faces of a balanced beam splitter at the same time leave together through one output face, never one through each. This holds provided they are identical in frequency, polarization and arrival time. Hong, Ou and Mandel observed it in 1987 as a dip to near zero in the coincidence rate of two detectors placed behind the outputs. The effect follows from the exchange symmetry of bosons."),
        ("p", r"The many-particle amplitude of identical bosons is unchanged when two particles are exchanged. In the occupation description of Fock space, creation operators of different modes commute, and one mode can hold any number $n=0,1,2,\dots$ of quanta. Let $a^\dagger$ and $b^\dagger$ create a photon in the two input modes, and $c^\dagger$ and $d^\dagger$ in the two output modes. A lossless 50:50 beam splitter acts linearly on the modes; with one choice of phases,"),
        ("eq", r"""a^\dagger\mapsto\frac{c^\dagger+d^\dagger}{\sqrt2},\qquad
b^\dagger\mapsto\frac{c^\dagger-d^\dagger}{\sqrt2},\\
a^\dagger b^\dagger\ket{0}\mapsto\frac12\left[(c^\dagger)^2-(d^\dagger)^2\right]\ket{0}
=\frac{\ket{2,0}-\ket{0,2}}{\sqrt2}."""),
        ("p", r"The cross terms $-c^\dagger d^\dagger+d^\dagger c^\dagger$ cancel because the operators commute. In terms of paths, both photons transmitted and both photons reflected lead to the same final state with amplitudes of equal magnitude and opposite sign. For fermions, anticommutation makes the same cross terms add while $(c^\dagger)^2=0$, so two identical fermions always leave through different faces. For distinguishable particles the probabilities add, and a coincidence occurs with probability one half."),
        ("p", r"Real photons are wave packets, and only the overlapping parts of their states interfere. Let the second photon be delayed by $\tau$, and let both have the normalized spectral intensity $|\phi(\omega)|^2$. For a Gaussian spectrum with root-mean-square angular-frequency width $\sigma$, the coincidence probability is"),
        ("eq", r"""P_c(\tau)=\frac12\left[1-\left|\int|\phi(\omega)|^2e^{i\omega\tau}d\omega\right|^2\right]
=\frac12\left(1-e^{-\sigma^2\tau^2}\right)."""),
        ("p", r"The width of the dip is set by the inverse bandwidth, not by the wavelength. For photons at 800 nm with a Gaussian spectrum of 5 nm full width at half maximum, $\sigma=6.2\times10^{12}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. The dip then has a full width of 270 fs at half depth, corresponding to about 80 micrometres of path. Translating one input mirror over this range traces the dip. Its depth, the visibility $V=1-P_c(0)/P_c(\infty)$, equals the squared overlap of the two single-photon states."),
        ("p", r"Visibility measurements therefore test whether photons from separate emitters are identical, a requirement for photonic quantum logic. The calculation assumes exactly one photon in each input. Two independent laser pulses with Poisson photon numbers give at most 50 percent visibility, so a deeper dip excludes a description by classical fields. Loss and finite detector efficiency reduce the coincidence rate by the same factor at every delay and leave the visibility unchanged."),
        ("p", r"Elementary bosons include the photon, the gluons, the W and Z bosons and the Higgs boson, all with integer spin. Composite particles with an even number of fermion constituents, such as helium-4 atoms, behave as bosons when an experiment cannot resolve their structure. The same commutation rule produces stimulated emission and the condensation described under Bose--Einstein statistics."),
    ],
    "electron": [
        ("p", r"An electron held in a magnetic field precesses its spin slightly faster than it circulates in its cyclotron orbit. The small difference between the two frequencies fixes the electron's magnetic moment with a relative uncertainty of about $10^{-13}$. Through the $g$-factor, it tests the Dirac equation and quantum electrodynamics together."),
        ("p", r"The electron has charge $-e$, mass $m=0.511\,\mathrm{MeV}/c^2$ and spin one half, with spin operator $\mathbf S=\hbar\boldsymbol\sigma/2$ built from the Pauli matrices $\boldsymbol\sigma$. Its magnetic moment is written $\boldsymbol\mu=-g(e/2m)\mathbf S$. A classical rotating body with equal distributions of charge and mass has $g=1$. In the nonrelativistic limit of the Dirac equation, the two upper spinor components obey a Schrödinger equation with kinetic energy $(\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\boldsymbol\pi)^2/2m$. Here $\boldsymbol\pi=\mathbf p+e\mathbf A$ is the kinetic momentum in a vector potential $\mathbf A$:"),
        ("eq", r"""(\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\boldsymbol\pi)^2=\boldsymbol\pi^2+e\hbar\,\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf B,
\qquad
H=\frac{\boldsymbol\pi^2}{2m}+2\,\frac{e}{2m}\,\mathbf S\cdot\mathbf B."""),
        ("p", r"The spin term arises because the components of $\boldsymbol\pi$ do not commute in a magnetic field $\mathbf B$: $[\pi_x,\pi_y]=-ie\hbar B_z$, and cyclically. Comparison with $H=-\boldsymbol\mu\cdot\mathbf B$ gives $g=2$. A Schrödinger equation with kinetic energy $\boldsymbol\pi^2/2m$ contains no such term, and the Dirac value is not inserted by hand. With $g=2$ the spin precession frequency $g\,eB/2m$ equals the cyclotron frequency $eB/m$, so the spin keeps a fixed angle to the velocity."),
        ("p", r"Coupling to the quantized electromagnetic field changes this value slightly. The electron emits and reabsorbs virtual photons, which modifies its response to a slowly varying external field. Schwinger's one-loop calculation of 1948 gave the anomaly $a_e=(g-2)/2=\alpha/2\pi\approx0.0011614$, with $\alpha$ the fine-structure constant. Higher orders form a series in $\alpha/\pi$ whose coefficients are known through the fifth power:"),
        ("eq", r"""a_e=\frac12\left(\frac{\alpha}{\pi}\right)-0.328479\left(\frac{\alpha}{\pi}\right)^2\\
+1.181241\left(\frac{\alpha}{\pi}\right)^3-1.9122\left(\frac{\alpha}{\pi}\right)^4+\dots"""),
        ("p", r"In a Penning trap the anomaly is measured directly as a ratio of frequencies. The cyclotron frequency is $\omega_c=eB/m$ and the spin precession frequency is $\omega_s=g\,eB/2m$, so their difference isolates $a_e$:"),
        ("eq", r"""\omega_a=\omega_s-\omega_c=a_e\,\omega_c,\qquad
\frac{g}{2}=1+\frac{\omega_a}{\omega_c}."""),
        ("p", r"At $B=5.36$ T, $\omega_c/2\pi=150$ GHz and $\omega_a/2\pi=174$ MHz. A single electron in a cryogenic trap occupies the lowest cyclotron levels, and quantum jumps between these levels and between spin states are detected one at a time. The 2023 measurement found $a_e=0.00115965218059(13)$. The series summed through $(\alpha/\pi)^5$ gives $0.00115965218$ with the value of $\alpha$ from atom-recoil experiments. The remaining difference, about $4\times10^{-12}$, is of the size of the calculated contributions from virtual muons and hadrons. At this level the comparison is limited by the independent measurements of $\alpha$, which disagree among themselves by more than their stated uncertainties."),
        ("p", r"The Pauli description with $g=2$ requires fields that vary slowly over the reduced Compton wavelength $\hbar/mc=0.386$ pm and kinetic energies small compared with $mc^2$. Corrections of relative order $(v/c)^2$ give the hydrogen fine structure derived under Dirac equation. The radiative effects that shift $g$ also shift atomic levels, and the Lamb shift discussed in Quantum electrodynamics is their counterpart in hydrogen."),
    ],
    "fermi_dirac_statistics": [
        ("p", r"The conduction electrons of copper, about one per atom, contribute only about one percent of the heat capacity that a classical gas of free particles would have at room temperature. The classical Drude model could not explain this. The explanation lies in the occupation rule derived in Quantum statistical mechanics. A fermionic state holds zero or one particle, and at temperature $T$ and chemical potential $\mu$ its mean occupation is $f(\epsilon)=[e^{(\epsilon-\mu)/k_BT}+1]^{-1}$."),
        ("p", r"At $T=0$ the function $f$ is a step. Every state below $E_F=\mu(0)$ is filled and every state above it is empty. For free electrons of mass $m$ with two spin states per wave vector, the filled states form a sphere of radius $k_F$ in wave-vector space. Counting one wave vector per volume $(2\pi)^3/V$ gives the density $n$, the Fermi energy and the density of states at the Fermi energy:"),
        ("eq", r"""n=\frac{k_F^3}{3\pi^2},\qquad
E_F=\frac{\hbar^2k_F^2}{2m}=\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}(3\pi^2n)^{2/3},\qquad
g(E_F)=\frac{3N}{2E_F}."""),
        ("p", r"Copper has $n=8.47\times10^{28}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$. This gives $k_F=1.36\times10^{10}\,\mathrm{m^{-1}}$, $E_F=7.0$ eV and a Fermi temperature $T_F=E_F/k_B=8.2\times10^4$ K. Electrons at the Fermi surface move at $1.6\times10^6\,\mathrm{m/s}$ even at absolute zero. The filled sphere exerts a degeneracy pressure $\frac25nE_F\approx38$ GPa without any repulsion between the electrons. Room temperature is a low temperature for this gas: $T/T_F\approx0.004$."),
        ("p", r"Heating can change occupations only within about $k_BT$ of $E_F$, because deeper electrons have no empty states nearby to move into. Roughly $g(E_F)k_BT$ electrons each gain an energy of order $k_BT$, so the energy rises as $T^2$ and the heat capacity is linear in $T$. The Sommerfeld expansion fixes the coefficient. For a function $H(\epsilon)$ that is smooth near $\mu$,"),
        ("eq", r"""\int_0^\infty H(\epsilon)f(\epsilon)\,d\epsilon=\int_0^\mu H(\epsilon)\,d\epsilon
+\frac{\pi^2}{6}(k_BT)^2H'(\mu)+O(T^4),\\
C_{\rm el}=\frac{\pi^2}{3}g(E_F)k_B^2T=\frac{\pi^2}{2}Nk_B\frac{T}{T_F}."""),
        ("p", r"Applying the expansion to the particle number gives the small downward shift of $\mu$ with temperature; applying it to the energy gives $C_{\rm el}$. Per mole of copper, $C_{\rm el}=\gamma T$ with $\gamma=0.50\,\mathrm{mJ\,mol^{-1}K^{-2}}$. At 300 K this is $0.018R$, where $R$ is the gas constant, compared with the classical value $3R/2$ for free particles and the lattice value $3R$."),
        ("p", r"Below a few kelvin the lattice contribution falls as $T^3$, and the electronic term dominates. A plot of $C/T$ against $T^2$ is then a straight line whose intercept is $\gamma$. For copper the measured intercept is $0.695\,\mathrm{mJ\,mol^{-1}K^{-2}}$, larger than the free-electron value by a factor of 1.38. The excess measures the density of states at the Fermi energy of the real band structure, enhanced by coupling to lattice vibrations. It is usually reported as a thermal effective mass."),
        ("p", r"The derivation assumes $k_BT\ll E_F$ and a density of states that is smooth on the scale $k_BT$. Electron-electron interactions change the coefficients but, in a Fermi liquid, keep the linear law. When $T\gg T_F$ all occupations are small and $f$ approaches the Maxwell--Boltzmann weight. The zero-or-one restriction comes from the antisymmetry discussed under Fermion. With a minus sign in the denominator instead, Bose--Einstein statistics allows macroscopic occupation of a single state."),
    ],
    "fermion": [
        ("p", r"Atoms of helium-3, released from a trap and detected far away, arrive in pairs at nearby points less often than uncorrelated particles would. Atoms of helium-4 in the same apparatus arrive together more often. Electrons with parallel spins in a metal similarly avoid one another, even apart from their Coulomb repulsion. These effects follow from the sign that the amplitude of identical fermions acquires under exchange."),
        ("p", r"Let $z=(\mathbf x,s)$ denote the position and spin projection of one particle. The amplitude of identical fermions changes sign when two complete coordinates are exchanged. With orthonormal one-particle states $\varphi_a,\varphi_b$ for two particles, or $\varphi_1,\dots,\varphi_N$ for $N$ particles, the antisymmetric amplitudes are"),
        ("eq", r"""\Psi(z_1,z_2)=\frac{\varphi_a(z_1)\varphi_b(z_2)-\varphi_b(z_1)\varphi_a(z_2)}{\sqrt2},\\
\Psi(z_1,\dots,z_N)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{N!}}\det\left[\varphi_i(z_j)\right]."""),
        ("p", r"A determinant with two equal rows or two equal columns vanishes. Hence $\Psi=0$ if two particles occupy the same one-particle state, which is the Pauli exclusion principle. The amplitude also vanishes whenever $z_1=z_2$, so two particles with equal spin projection are never found at the same point. Opposite spin projections make the complete coordinates different. Two electrons can therefore share one spatial orbital in a spin singlet, as in the helium ground state or the chemical bond treated under Quantum chemistry."),
        ("p", r"The suppression extends over a finite distance. Consider the ground state of free electrons in a large volume, a Slater determinant of plane waves filling a Fermi sphere of radius $k_F$. Let $n_\sigma=k_F^3/6\pi^2$ be the density of one spin orientation. The probability density of finding a same-spin electron at distance $r$ from a given one, divided by $n_\sigma$, is"),
        ("eq", r"""g_{\uparrow\uparrow}(r)=1-\left[\frac{3j_1(k_Fr)}{k_Fr}\right]^2,\qquad
j_1(x)=\frac{\sin x-x\cos x}{x^2},\\
n_\sigma\int\left[g_{\uparrow\uparrow}(r)-1\right]d^3r=-1."""),
        ("p", r"The bracket is the average of $e^{i\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf r}$ over the filled sphere and comes from the exchange terms of the determinant. The function $g_{\uparrow\uparrow}$ rises from zero at $r=0$ to one half at $k_Fr=1.81$ and approaches one with weak oscillations. The integral shows that this exchange hole contains exactly one missing electron of the same spin. Opposite spins remain uncorrelated in this state, $g_{\uparrow\downarrow}=1$, until the Coulomb interaction is included."),
        ("p", r"In sodium, with one conduction electron per atom, $k_F=0.92\times10^{10}\,\mathrm{m^{-1}}$, and the same-spin density falls to half within 0.20 nm of each electron. Each electron is thus surrounded by a deficit of negative charge, which lowers its electrostatic energy. For the uniform electron gas this exchange energy is $-(3/4\pi)\,e^2k_F/4\pi\epsilon_0$ per electron. In Rydberg units it is $-0.916/r_s$, with $r_s$ the Wigner--Seitz radius in Bohr radii. Sodium has $r_s=3.93$, which gives $-3.2$ eV per electron, larger in magnitude than the mean kinetic energy of 1.9 eV."),
        ("p", r"The exchange sign is not an interaction and has no adjustable strength; it is fixed by the identity of the particles. Its connection with half-integer spin, the spin-statistics theorem, requires relativistic quantum field theory with locality and positive energy. In the occupation description of Fock space the same rule is the anticommutation $\{a_i^\dagger,a_j^\dagger\}=0$, so that $(a_i^\dagger)^2=0$. Thermal occupations of fermionic states follow Fermi--Dirac statistics. Quasiparticles confined to two dimensions can have other exchange phases."),
    ],
    "fock_space": [
        ("p", r"An excited atom in an empty cavity can decay to its ground state, leaving one photon in a cavity mode. The light of a laser contains no definite number of photons at all. A description with a fixed number of particles cannot represent either situation. Fock space collects the states of zero, one, two and more identical particles in one Hilbert space, so that a single operator can change the particle number."),
        ("p", r"Let $\mathcal H$ be the one-particle Hilbert space with orthonormal modes $\varphi_1,\varphi_2,\dots$. The $N$-particle space is the symmetric part of $\mathcal H^{\otimes N}$ for bosons and the antisymmetric part for fermions, written $S_\pm\mathcal H^{\otimes N}$. Fock space is their direct sum. Its basis states are labeled by occupation numbers $n_i$, the number of particles in mode $i$:"),
        ("eq", r"""\mathcal F_\pm=\bigoplus_{N=0}^{\infty}S_\pm\mathcal H^{\otimes N},\qquad
\ket{n_1,n_2,\dots},\qquad \hat N=\sum_ia_i^\dagger a_i."""),
        ("p", r"For bosons the creation and annihilation operators change one occupation by one unit, with factors fixed by the commutation relations:"),
        ("eq", r"""a_i^\dagger\ket{\dots,n_i,\dots}=\sqrt{n_i+1}\,\ket{\dots,n_i+1,\dots},\\
a_i\ket{\dots,n_i,\dots}=\sqrt{n_i}\,\ket{\dots,n_i-1,\dots},\qquad
[a_i,a_j^\dagger]=\delta_{ij}."""),
        ("p", r"For fermions $n_i$ is zero or one, the commutator becomes an anticommutator, and each operator carries a sign $(-1)^{\sum_{k<i}n_k}$ fixed by an ordering of the modes. Exchange symmetry is then built in: a state specifies how many particles occupy each mode, not which particle is where. A one-body operator with matrix elements $h_{ij}=\langle\varphi_i|h|\varphi_j\rangle$ becomes $\sum_{ij}h_{ij}a_i^\dagger a_j$, the same expression for every particle number. Processes that create or absorb particles contain unequal numbers of creation and annihilation operators."),
        ("p", r"The emission of light shows what the factor $\sqrt{n+1}$ does. Consider a two-level atom with ground state $g$ and excited state $e$, coupled to one field mode of angular frequency $\omega$ that contains $n$ photons. Near resonance, in the rotating-wave approximation, the coupling is $\hbar\lambda(\sigma_+a+\sigma_-a^\dagger)$. Here $\sigma_-$ takes the atom from $e$ to $g$, $\sigma_+$ does the reverse, and $\lambda$ is a coupling frequency. The ladder relations give"),
        ("eq", r"""\langle g,n+1|H_{\rm int}|e,n\rangle=\hbar\lambda\sqrt{n+1},\qquad
\langle e,n-1|H_{\rm int}|g,n\rangle=\hbar\lambda\sqrt{n}."""),
        ("p", r"Transition rates are proportional to squared matrix elements. Emission into the mode therefore proceeds at a rate proportional to $n+1$, and absorption at a rate proportional to $n$. The part proportional to $n$ is stimulated emission; the remaining term is spontaneous emission, present even in an empty mode. This is Einstein's relation between his A and B coefficients, here a consequence of the operator algebra. With equilibrium populations $N_e/N_g=e^{-\hbar\omega/k_BT}$, the balance $N_e(n+1)=N_gn$ gives $n=(e^{\hbar\omega/k_BT}-1)^{-1}$, the Planck occupation."),
        ("p", r"The ratio of stimulated to spontaneous emission into a mode equals its occupation. For visible light at 600 nm in surroundings at 300 K, the thermal occupation is $n\approx2\times10^{-35}$, so thermal sources emit spontaneously. A 1 GHz microwave mode at the same temperature has $n\approx6\times10^3$, and stimulated processes dominate. A laser maintains a large $n$ in one mode by population inversion, which is why its emission is coherent and directional."),
        ("p", r"A fixed set of modes describes noninteracting particles exactly. Interactions mix occupation states, so the occupation basis remains a basis but its states are no longer stationary. Each mode of a free field is a Quantum harmonic oscillator, and Quantum field theory uses Fock space as the state space of free particles. Bose--Einstein statistics and Fermi--Dirac statistics give the equilibrium distributions of the occupation numbers."),
    ],
    "photon": [
        ("p", r"Light from an attenuated laser falling on a 50:50 beam splitter occasionally produces simultaneous clicks in detectors behind both outputs. Light emitted by a single atom or quantum dot after one excitation does not: one detector clicks, never both. Grangier, Roger and Aspect observed this anticorrelation in 1986 with photons from an atomic cascade, using the partner photon of each pair to announce the event. The quantity that distinguishes the two cases is the second-order correlation $g^{(2)}(0)$."),
        ("p", r"The electromagnetic field in a volume $V$ is a set of modes labeled by a wave vector $\mathbf k$ and a transverse polarization vector $\boldsymbol\epsilon_\lambda$, with $\mathbf k\cdot\boldsymbol\epsilon_\lambda=0$. Each mode is a harmonic oscillator with angular frequency $\omega=c|\mathbf k|$ and annihilation operator $a_{\mathbf k\lambda}$, where $[a,a^\dagger]=1$. A photon is one quantum of such a mode:"),
        ("eq", r"""E=\hbar\omega,\qquad \mathbf p=\hbar\mathbf k,\qquad
\ket{1_{\mathbf k\lambda}}=a_{\mathbf k\lambda}^\dagger\ket{0},\\
\mathbf E^{(+)}(\mathbf r,t)=i\sum_{\mathbf k,\lambda}\sqrt{\frac{\hbar\omega}{2\epsilon_0V}}\,
\boldsymbol\epsilon_\lambda a_{\mathbf k\lambda}e^{i(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t)}."""),
        ("p", r"Here $\mathbf E^{(+)}$ is the positive-frequency part of the electric field operator, and $\epsilon_0$ is the vacuum permittivity. A one-photon state has zero mean electric field, although it carries energy $\hbar\omega$ above the vacuum. An absorbing detector registers normally ordered products of field operators. For two detectors viewing the same mode, the ratio of the coincidence rate to the product of the single rates at zero delay is"),
        ("eq", r"""g^{(2)}(0)=\frac{\langle a^\dagger a^\dagger aa\rangle}{\langle a^\dagger a\rangle^2}
=\frac{\langle n(n-1)\rangle}{\langle n\rangle^2}."""),
        ("p", r"For a number state $|n\rangle$ this equals $1-1/n$, which vanishes for one photon. A coherent state, the output of an ideal laser, has a Poisson number distribution and $g^{(2)}(0)=1$. Thermal light has $g^{(2)}(0)=2$, the bunching measured by Hanbury Brown and Twiss. A classical fluctuating intensity $I$ gives $\langle I^2\rangle/\langle I\rangle^2$, which the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality keeps at or above one. A value below one therefore has no description by a classical wave."),
        ("p", r"A two-level emitter, such as a semiconductor quantum dot, must be excited again after each emission before it can emit another photon. Under pulsed excitation the coincidence histogram consists of peaks separated by the pulse period. The value of $g^{(2)}(0)$ is the area of the peak at zero delay divided by the area of a distant peak. If a pulse yields one photon with probability $p_1$ and two photons with a small probability $p_2$, then $g^{(2)}(0)\approx2p_2/p_1^2$. Quantum dots in optical microcavities reach values of order 0.01 and below."),
        ("p", r"An attenuated laser behaves differently. With mean photon number $\bar n$ per pulse, the ratio of two-photon to one-photon pulses is $\bar n/2$, and $g^{(2)}(0)$ remains 1. Multiphoton events can then be suppressed only by reducing the rate of single photons as well. This difference matters in quantum key distribution, where a second photon in a pulse can leak information."),
        ("p", r"Linear loss removes photons independently and leaves $g^{(2)}(0)$ unchanged. Background light, detector dark counts and timing jitter raise the measured value above that of the emitted state. A small $g^{(2)}(0)$ certifies few multiphoton events but not that successive photons are identical. That property is tested by the Hong--Ou--Mandel interference described under Boson. The temporal shape of each photon is set by the spontaneous decay treated under Quantum jump."),
    ],
    "quantum_electrodynamics": [
        ("p", r"In the Dirac theory of hydrogen, the $2S_{1/2}$ and $2P_{1/2}$ levels have exactly equal energies. In 1947 Lamb and Retherford drove microwave transitions between them and found $2S_{1/2}$ higher by about 1000 MHz. The shift arises because the electron is coupled to the quantized electromagnetic field. Quantum electrodynamics treats that field as a dynamical quantum system rather than as a prescribed potential."),
        ("p", r"In natural units $\hbar=c=1$, let $\psi$ be the electron field with mass $m$ and charge $q=-e$, and let $A_\mu$ be the electromagnetic potential. The theory is defined by the Lagrangian density"),
        ("eq", r"""\mathcal L_{\rm QED}=-\frac14F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}+\bar\psi(i\gamma^\mu D_\mu-m)\psi,\\
D_\mu=\partial_\mu+iqA_\mu,\qquad F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu."""),
        ("p", r"Invariance under the local phase change $\psi\to e^{-iq\chi}\psi$, together with $A_\mu\to A_\mu+\partial_\mu\chi$, fixes the coupling term $-qA_\mu\bar\psi\gamma^\mu\psi$. The current $j^\mu=q\bar\psi\gamma^\mu\psi$ is conserved and is the source in Maxwell's equations, $\partial_\mu F^{\mu\nu}=j^\nu$. Gauge invariance also implies the Ward--Takahashi identity, which makes charge renormalization the same for every charged particle. Perturbation theory in $\alpha=e^2/4\pi\approx1/137$ describes emission, absorption and exchange of photons. For a bound electron, emission followed by reabsorption of a photon shifts the energy."),
        ("p", r"Welton's estimate shows where the shift comes from. Zero-point fluctuations of the field shake the electron, so it samples the Coulomb potential $V$ over a small random displacement $\delta\mathbf r$. Averaging $V$ over that motion adds $\frac16\langle\delta r^2\rangle\nabla^2V$. A field mode of angular frequency $\omega$ drives a displacement proportional to $1/\omega^2$, and the sum over modes gives a logarithm. With $\hbar$ and $c$ restored,"),
        ("eq", r"""\langle\delta r^2\rangle=\frac{2\alpha}{\pi}\left(\frac{\hbar}{mc}\right)^2
\ln\frac{\omega_{\max}}{\omega_{\min}},\\
\Delta E_{2S}=\frac16\langle\delta r^2\rangle\langle\nabla^2V\rangle
=\frac{\alpha^5mc^2}{6\pi}\ln\frac{\omega_{\max}}{\omega_{\min}}."""),
        ("p", r"The last step uses $\nabla^2V=4\pi\alpha\hbar c\,\delta^3(\mathbf r)$ for the Coulomb potential and $|\psi_{2S}(0)|^2=1/8\pi a_0^3$, with $a_0$ the Bohr radius. Only S states have a nonzero density at the nucleus, so $2P_{1/2}$ is not shifted at this order. The upper limit is $\hbar\omega_{\max}\approx mc^2$, where the nonrelativistic treatment fails. The lower limit is set by atomic binding, because the electron does not respond freely to slower fields. Bethe's 1947 calculation used an average excitation energy of about 17.8 Rydberg, or 242 eV."),
        ("p", r"With $\alpha^5mc^2/6\pi h=135.6$ MHz and a logarithm of 7.65, the estimate is about 1040 MHz, with $2S_{1/2}$ above $2P_{1/2}$. The modern value, 1057.8 MHz, requires a relativistic treatment of the high-frequency modes and further terms. In vacuum polarization, virtual electron-positron pairs screen the proton's charge beyond the electron Compton wavelength, and this lowers $2S$ by 27 MHz. The anomalous magnetic moment of the Electron and nuclear recoil also contribute."),
        ("p", r"Once the electron mass and charge are fixed by measurement, every term is finite; the procedure is described under Renormalization. The expansion assumes $Z\alpha\ll1$. In heavy hydrogen-like ions the electron must be treated to all orders in the nuclear field, and the same theory is tested there in a strong-field regime."),
    ],
    "quantum_field_theory": [
        ("p", r"A mirror moving at a speed close to that of light emits photons even when no light was present before. In 2011 Wilson and coworkers observed this dynamical Casimir effect in a superconducting circuit, where microwave photons appeared in correlated pairs. Two features of quantum field theory explain it: particle number is not conserved, and the vacuum depends on the Hamiltonian."),
        ("p", r"Consider a real scalar field $\phi(\mathbf x,t)$ in a box of volume $V$, in units with $\hbar=c=1$. Its conjugate momentum is $\pi=\dot\phi$, and its energy contains kinetic, gradient and mass terms. Expanding in plane waves decouples the field into one oscillator for each wave vector $\mathbf k$:"),
        ("eq", r"""H=\int d^3x\left[\frac{\pi^2}{2}+\frac{(\nabla\phi)^2}{2}+\frac{m^2\phi^2}{2}\right]
=\sum_{\mathbf k}\omega_{\mathbf k}\left(a_{\mathbf k}^\dagger a_{\mathbf k}+\frac12\right),\\
\phi(\mathbf x)=\sum_{\mathbf k}\frac{a_{\mathbf k}e^{i\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf x}
+a_{\mathbf k}^\dagger e^{-i\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf x}}{\sqrt{2\omega_{\mathbf k}V}},\qquad
\omega_{\mathbf k}=\sqrt{\mathbf k^2+m^2}."""),
        ("p", r"The operators obey $[a_{\mathbf k},a_{\mathbf k'}^\dagger]=\delta_{\mathbf k\mathbf k'}$. Each mode is a Quantum harmonic oscillator, and its occupation number counts particles of momentum $\mathbf k$ and energy $\omega_{\mathbf k}$. States with any number of particles form a Fock space. The vacuum is the state annihilated by every $a_{\mathbf k}$, so it is defined with respect to these particular oscillators. Correlation functions such as $\langle0|T\{\phi(x)\phi(y)\}|0\rangle$ describe the propagation of a quantum between two spacetime points and are the elements of perturbative scattering amplitudes."),
        ("p", r"A single mode shows what happens when the Hamiltonian changes. Let a mode of unit mass have angular frequency $\omega_0$ for $t<0$ and be prepared in its ground state, which $a$ annihilates. At $t=0$ the frequency jumps to $\omega_1$. An instantaneous change leaves the state unchanged, but the new Hamiltonian is diagonalized by different operators. Expressing the coordinate and momentum through $a$ and $a^\dagger$ gives the Bogoliubov transformation and the mean number of new quanta:"),
        ("eq", r"""b=u\,a+v\,a^\dagger,\qquad
u=\frac{\omega_0+\omega_1}{2\sqrt{\omega_0\omega_1}},\qquad
v=\frac{\omega_1-\omega_0}{2\sqrt{\omega_0\omega_1}},\\
\langle n_1\rangle=\langle0|b^\dagger b|0\rangle=v^2=\frac{(\omega_0-\omega_1)^2}{4\omega_0\omega_1}."""),
        ("p", r"The relation $u^2-v^2=1$ preserves $[b,b^\dagger]=1$. The old ground state is not the new one: it contains new quanta, always in even numbers, because the transformation mixes $a$ with $a^\dagger$. Halving the frequency gives $\langle n_1\rangle=1/8$, with probability 0.943 of finding no quanta and 0.052 of finding two. The mean energy $\omega_1(\langle n_1\rangle+\frac12)$ exceeds the new zero-point energy, and the difference is the work done by whatever changed the frequency. If the frequency changes slowly, with $|\dot\omega|\ll\omega^2$, the mode follows its instantaneous ground state and no quanta appear."),
        ("p", r"In a field, a change that is uniform in space, such as a sudden change of the mass $m$, conserves momentum. It therefore mixes each $a_{\mathbf k}$ only with $a_{-\mathbf k}^\dagger$:"),
        ("eq", r"""b_{\mathbf k}=u_{\mathbf k}a_{\mathbf k}+v_{\mathbf k}a_{-\mathbf k}^\dagger,\qquad
\langle n_{\mathbf k}\rangle=|v_{\mathbf k}|^2,\qquad
u_{\mathbf k}^2-|v_{\mathbf k}|^2=1."""),
        ("p", r"Quanta are created in pairs of opposite momentum, so the total momentum stays zero while the total number changes. A periodic modulation at angular frequency $\Omega$ acts as a parametric drive and creates pairs whose frequencies add up to $\Omega$. In cosmology the same transformation describes the creation of field quanta by the expansion of space."),
        ("p", r"In the Wilson experiment a coplanar microwave waveguide is terminated by a SQUID, whose inductance depends on the magnetic flux through it. Modulating the flux near 11 GHz moves the effective end of the line back and forth at a few percent of the speed of light in the line. A material mirror cannot approach such speeds, and for small amplitudes the number of created quanta grows as the square of the speed ratio. The circuit was cooled so that thermal photons were negligible. The emitted radiation was symmetric about half the drive frequency, and its two-mode squeezing showed that photons at $\omega$ and $\Omega-\omega$ were produced together, as pair creation requires."),
        ("p", r"The static attraction between conducting plates, which arises from the zero-point energy of the same modes, is treated under Quantum fluctuation. These calculations assume free fields. With interactions the vacuum itself becomes a correlated state, and Renormalization relates the parameters of the theory to measured masses and charges."),
    ],
    "renormalization": [
        ("p", r"The strength of the electromagnetic interaction depends on the momentum transfer at which it is measured. Atomic experiments give $\alpha=1/137.036$, while electron-positron scattering at the Z-boson mass of 91 GeV requires $\alpha\approx1/129$. The difference is calculable. Virtual charged pairs screen a charge, and a probe with larger momentum transfer penetrates more of the screening cloud. Renormalization organizes this dependence on scale."),
        ("p", r"In quantum electrodynamics, loops of charged fermions modify the photon propagator, an effect called vacuum polarization. The interaction between two charges at momentum transfer $Q$ keeps its lowest-order form, with a modified coupling. Let fermion $f$ have mass $m_f$, charge $e_fe$ and $N_c$ colours. For $Q\gg m_f$, and with $\alpha$ defined at zero momentum transfer, the one-loop result is"),
        ("eq", r"""\alpha(Q)=\frac{\alpha}{1-\Delta\alpha(Q)},\\
\Delta\alpha(Q)=\frac{\alpha}{3\pi}\sum_fN_ce_f^2\left[\ln\frac{Q^2}{m_f^2}-\frac53\right]."""),
        ("p", r"Differentiating with respect to $\ln Q$ removes the constants and gives the rate of change of the coupling, the beta function. Integrating it between a reference scale $\mu$ and $Q$ gives"),
        ("eq", r"""\mu\frac{d\alpha}{d\mu}=\beta(\alpha)=\frac{2\alpha^2}{3\pi}\sum_fN_ce_f^2,\\
\frac{1}{\alpha(Q)}=\frac{1}{\alpha(\mu)}-\frac{2}{3\pi}\sum_fN_ce_f^2\ln\frac{Q}{\mu}."""),
        ("p", r"The positive beta function expresses screening: the effective charge grows at short distance. Only fermions lighter than $Q$ contribute appreciably, while heavier ones decouple, so the sum changes at each mass threshold. A prediction written in terms of $\alpha(Q)$ does not depend on the reference scale $\mu$ used to define the coupling. This independence is the content of the renormalization group equation."),
        ("p", r"The three charged leptons give $\Delta\alpha_{\rm lep}(M_Z)=0.0314$ at one loop. Quarks are confined at low momentum, so their contribution is obtained from the measured cross section for $e^+e^-\to\mathrm{hadrons}$ through a dispersion relation, which gives $\Delta\alpha_{\rm had}\approx0.0277$. Together they yield $1/\alpha(M_Z)\approx137.036\times(1-0.059)\approx128.9$. Bhabha scattering at LEP observed the predicted increase of the coupling directly. This running value enters the W-mass relation of the Standard Model."),
        ("p", r"Wilson's formulation explains why the same structure governs very different systems. Integrating out fluctuations between a cutoff $\Lambda$ and $\Lambda/b$ changes the couplings $g_i$ of the remaining long-wavelength theory. Near a fixed point $g^*$, where all beta functions vanish, small deviations scale with powers of $b$:"),
        ("eq", r"""\beta_i(g^*)=0,\qquad \delta g_i(b)=b^{y_i}\,\delta g_i(1)."""),
        ("p", r"Directions with $y_i>0$ grow and are called relevant; those with $y_i<0$ shrink and are called irrelevant. Long-distance behaviour then depends only on the few relevant couplings. For this reason a liquid near its critical point and a uniaxial magnet share the same critical exponents."),
        ("p", r"The one-loop QED formula holds while $(\alpha/3\pi)\ln(Q^2/m^2)$ remains small. Taken literally with the electron loop alone, it would make $\alpha$ diverge near $10^{280}$ MeV, far beyond the range where the theory is applied. In Quantum chromodynamics the self-interaction of gluons reverses the sign of the beta function, and the strong coupling decreases at short distance."),
    ],
    "dirac_equation": [
        ("p", r"The $n=2$ level of hydrogen is not a single level. Microwave and optical spectroscopy resolve components about 11 GHz apart, a fraction of about $10^{-5}$ of the binding energy of that level. Dirac's relativistic equation for the electron accounts for this fine structure and shows that the electron carries spin one half."),
        ("p", r"A relativistic wave equation must reproduce $E^2=p^2c^2+m^2c^4$. The Klein--Gordon equation does so with a second time derivative, and its conserved density can be negative. Dirac required instead an equation linear in $\partial_t$, $i\hbar\partial_t\psi=H\psi$, with a Hamiltonian linear in the momentum $\mathbf p$:"),
        ("eq", r"""H=c\,\boldsymbol\alpha\cdot\mathbf p+\beta mc^2,\\
\alpha_i\alpha_j+\alpha_j\alpha_i=2\delta_{ij},\qquad
\alpha_i\beta+\beta\alpha_i=0,\qquad \beta^2=1."""),
        ("p", r"Squaring gives $H^2=c^2p^2+m^2c^4$ exactly when these anticommutation relations hold. The smallest matrices that satisfy them are $4\times4$, so $\psi$ has four components. The density $\psi^\dagger\psi$ is positive and obeys a continuity equation. The free spectrum still contains negative energies $-\sqrt{p^2c^2+m^2c^4}$; in Quantum field theory these solutions describe positrons."),
        ("p", r"Rotational symmetry then requires spin. The orbital angular momentum $\mathbf L=\mathbf r\times\mathbf p$ does not commute with $H$; instead $[H,\mathbf L]=-i\hbar c\,\boldsymbol\alpha\times\mathbf p$. Let $\boldsymbol\Sigma$ be the $4\times4$ matrix made of two copies of the Pauli matrices. It satisfies $[H,\hbar\boldsymbol\Sigma/2]=i\hbar c\,\boldsymbol\alpha\times\mathbf p$, so the conserved angular momentum is"),
        ("eq", r"""\mathbf J=\mathbf L+\frac{\hbar}{2}\boldsymbol\Sigma,\qquad [H,\mathbf J]=0,\qquad
\left(\frac{\hbar}{2}\boldsymbol\Sigma\right)^2=\frac34\hbar^2."""),
        ("p", r"The internal part has the eigenvalue $s(s+1)\hbar^2$ with $s=1/2$. Spin one half is therefore a consequence of the linear relativistic equation, not an additional postulate. Coupling to a magnetic field in the same equation gives the magnetic moment with $g=2$, as described under Electron."),
        ("p", r"For an electron bound to a point nucleus of charge $Ze$, the Dirac equation with the Coulomb potential can be solved exactly. With $\alpha$ the fine-structure constant, the energy depends on the principal quantum number $n$ and on the total angular momentum $j$, but not separately on the orbital quantum number $l$:"),
        ("eq", r"""E_{nj}=mc^2\left[1+\left(\frac{Z\alpha}{n-j-\frac12+\sqrt{(j+\frac12)^2-Z^2\alpha^2}}\right)^2\right]^{-1/2}\\
\approx mc^2-\frac{mc^2(Z\alpha)^2}{2n^2}
-\frac{mc^2(Z\alpha)^4}{2n^4}\left(\frac{n}{j+\frac12}-\frac34\right)."""),
        ("p", r"The second term is the Bohr energy. The third is smaller by a factor of order $(Z\alpha)^2\approx5\times10^{-5}$ for hydrogen and gives the fine structure. In a nonrelativistic expansion it combines three effects: the relativistic correction to the kinetic energy, spin-orbit coupling with $g=2$, and the Darwin term for S states. For $n=2$ the levels with $j=1/2$ lie below those with $j=3/2$ by $mc^2\alpha^4/32$. With the reduced-mass correction this is 10.943 GHz, compared with the measured $2P_{3/2}$--$2P_{1/2}$ interval of 10.969 GHz."),
        ("p", r"The remaining 0.2 percent comes mainly from the anomalous magnetic moment of the electron, which multiplies the spin-orbit term by $1+2a_e$. The formula also predicts that $2S_{1/2}$ and $2P_{1/2}$, which share $n=2$ and $j=1/2$, coincide. Their measured separation of 1057.8 MHz, the Lamb shift, is calculated in Quantum electrodynamics. For a point nucleus the solution with $j=1/2$ fails when $Z\alpha>1$. With a finite nucleus the lowest level reaches the negative-energy continuum near $Z\approx170$, where a single-particle equation no longer applies."),
    ],
    "klein_gordon_equation": [
        ("p", r"Nuclear forces fall to negligible strength within a few femtometres, whereas the Coulomb force has unlimited range. In 1935 Yukawa attributed the short range to the mass of an exchanged particle, later identified as the pion. The relation between mass and range follows from the Klein--Gordon equation, the relativistic wave equation for a particle without spin."),
        ("p", r"Replacing $E$ by $i\hbar\partial_t$ and $\mathbf p$ by $-i\hbar\nabla$ in $E^2=p^2c^2+m^2c^4$ gives an equation for a field $\phi(\mathbf x,t)$. Plane waves solve it for either sign of the energy:"),
        ("eq", r"""\left(\frac{1}{c^2}\frac{\partial^2}{\partial t^2}-\nabla^2+\frac{m^2c^2}{\hbar^2}\right)\phi=0,\\
\phi=e^{i(\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf x-Et)/\hbar},\qquad E=\pm\sqrt{p^2c^2+m^2c^4}."""),
        ("p", r"The negative-energy solutions have no lower bound. A particle described by them could, once coupled to the electromagnetic field, radiate without limit. A second difficulty concerns probability. The equation implies a continuity equation $\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot\mathbf j=0$, with"),
        ("eq", r"""\rho=\frac{i\hbar}{2mc^2}\left(\phi^*\partial_t\phi-\phi\,\partial_t\phi^*\right),\qquad
\mathbf j=\frac{\hbar}{2mi}\left(\phi^*\nabla\phi-\phi\nabla\phi^*\right),\\
\rho=\frac{E}{mc^2}|\phi|^2\quad\text{for a plane wave}."""),
        ("p", r"For negative-energy plane waves $\rho$ is negative. More generally, because the equation is second order in time, $\phi$ and $\partial_t\phi$ can be chosen independently at an initial instant, and $\rho$ can be made negative anywhere. It is therefore not a probability density. Pauli and Weisskopf resolved this in 1934 by treating $\phi$ as a quantized field. Multiplied by the charge, $\rho$ becomes a charge density whose sign distinguishes particles from antiparticles. Positive-frequency solutions describe $\pi^+$ mesons, and negative-frequency solutions describe $\pi^-$ mesons with positive energy."),
        ("p", r"For a static point source of strength $g$ the field obeys $(\nabla^2-\mu^2)\phi=-g\,\delta^3(\mathbf r)$, with $\mu=mc/\hbar$. The solution falls off exponentially, and a second nucleon coupled to the same field acquires a potential energy proportional to it:"),
        ("eq", r"""\phi(r)=\frac{g\,e^{-\mu r}}{4\pi r},\qquad
V(r)\propto-\frac{e^{-r/\lambda}}{r},\qquad \lambda=\frac{\hbar}{mc}."""),
        ("p", r"The range is the reduced Compton wavelength of the exchanged particle. With the charged-pion mass $139.57\,\mathrm{MeV}/c^2$ and $\hbar c=197.33$ MeV fm, $\lambda=1.41$ fm. Nucleon-nucleon scattering in partial waves of high angular momentum, which probe large separations, follows the one-pion-exchange potential. Because the pion is pseudoscalar, that potential also contains spin and isospin factors and a tensor part, but its central part has this Yukawa form. Below about 1 fm, heavier mesons and the quark structure of nucleons take over."),
        ("p", r"The Klein--Gordon equation with a Coulomb potential also describes pionic atoms, in which a $\pi^-$ replaces an electron. Their X-ray transition energies are used to determine the charged-pion mass. As a single-particle equation it applies when potentials change slowly over $\lambda$ and energies remain well below the pair-creation threshold $2mc^2$. For particles of spin one half it is replaced by the Dirac equation, although each component of a free Dirac spinor still satisfies it. In Quantum field theory every free relativistic field obeys it, as the dispersion relation of each mode."),
    ],
    "quantum_chemistry": [
        ("p", r"A proton and a hydrogen atom bind into the molecular ion $\mathrm H_2^+$. The bond is held by a single electron, and the depth of the potential well is 2.79 eV. The binding comes from the wave nature of the electron: its amplitudes near the two nuclei interfere. The simplest calculation that shows this, the linear combination of atomic orbitals, is also the starting point of molecular-orbital theory."),
        ("p", r"Protons are 1836 times heavier than electrons and move much more slowly. In the Born--Oppenheimer approximation they are held at a fixed separation $R$. The electronic energy at each $R$, including the proton repulsion, then serves as the potential for nuclear motion. In atomic units the electronic Hamiltonian is $H=-\tfrac12\nabla^2-1/r_a-1/r_b+1/R$, with $r_a$ and $r_b$ the distances to the two protons. The trial states combine the hydrogen 1s orbitals $\phi_a$ and $\phi_b$ centred on each proton:"),
        ("eq", r"""\psi_\pm=\frac{\phi_a\pm\phi_b}{\sqrt{2(1\pm S)}},\qquad
E_\pm(R)=-\frac12+\frac1R-\frac{J\pm K}{1\pm S}."""),
        ("p", r"The variational principle selects these combinations. The $2\times2$ matrices of $H$ and of the overlap in the basis $\phi_a,\phi_b$ are unchanged when the protons are interchanged, so their eigenvectors are the even and odd combinations. Each $E_\pm$ is an upper bound to the exact energy of that symmetry. The three integrals are the overlap $S=\langle\phi_a|\phi_b\rangle$, the attraction $J=\langle\phi_a|r_b^{-1}|\phi_a\rangle$ of one atomic cloud to the other proton, and the resonance integral $K=\langle\phi_a|r_a^{-1}|\phi_b\rangle$. The last exists only because the orbitals overlap:"),
        ("eq", r"""S=e^{-R}\left(1+R+\frac{R^2}{3}\right),\qquad
J=\frac1R-e^{-2R}\left(1+\frac1R\right),\qquad
K=e^{-R}(1+R)."""),
        ("p", r"In the even, bonding state the electron density accumulates between the protons, where both attract it. In the odd, antibonding state it has a node on the midplane. Minimizing $E_+(R)$ gives an equilibrium separation of 2.49 bohr, or 0.132 nm, and a well depth of 1.76 eV below a hydrogen atom and a free proton. The exact Born--Oppenheimer solution gives 2.00 bohr, or 0.106 nm, and 2.79 eV. Letting the orbital exponent vary with $R$ already improves the result to 2.00 bohr and 2.35 eV."),
        ("p", r"The antibonding curve $E_-(R)$ is repulsive at all separations. Light that promotes the electron from the bonding to the antibonding state therefore dissociates the ion into a proton and a hydrogen atom. The two-orbital problem is a Two-state quantum system: the coupling between two localized states splits them into symmetric and antisymmetric combinations, as at an avoided crossing. The Born--Oppenheimer separation fails where two electronic energy surfaces approach each other, as in many photochemical reactions."),
        ("p", r"In $\mathrm H_2$ two electrons occupy the bonding orbital with opposite spins, in a singlet. The antisymmetry discussed under Fermion forces additional electrons into the antibonding orbital. With four electrons, as in $\mathrm{He}_2$, bonding and antibonding contributions cancel and no chemical bond forms. Electron repulsion makes the single-orbital description of $\mathrm H_2$ inaccurate at large separation, where it gives equal weight to ionic and covalent configurations."),
        ("p", r"Configuration interaction and coupled-cluster methods add this correlation systematically. Density-functional theory includes it through an approximate functional of the electron density. Each computes the same Born--Oppenheimer energy surface. The cost of an exact calculation grows exponentially with the number of orbitals, so strongly correlated molecules, such as many transition-metal complexes, remain difficult. Computing such energies is one proposed application of quantum computers; demonstrations so far concern small molecules."),
    ],
    "standard_model": [
        ("p", r"The masses of the W and Z bosons, 80.37 and 91.19 GeV in units with $c=1$, are not independent quantities. Their ratio is fixed by one angle, which also determines how the Z boson couples to electrons and neutrinos. This constraint, tested at electron-positron and hadron colliders, follows from the gauge structure and particle content of the Standard Model."),
        ("p", r"The Standard Model is a gauge theory with group $SU(3)_c\times SU(2)_L\times U(1)_Y$. The factor $SU(3)_c$ acts on colour and is the subject of Quantum chromodynamics. The electroweak factors act differently on the two chiralities of each fermion: left-handed fields form $SU(2)_L$ doublets and right-handed fields are singlets. One generation, listed with its representation of $SU(3)_c\times SU(2)_L$ and hypercharge $Y$ as a subscript, is"),
        ("eq", r"""Q_L=\begin{pmatrix}u_L\\d_L\end{pmatrix}:(3,2)_{1/6},\qquad
u_R:(3,1)_{2/3},\qquad d_R:(3,1)_{-1/3},\\
L_L=\begin{pmatrix}\nu_L\\e_L\end{pmatrix}:(1,2)_{-1/2},\qquad
e_R:(1,1)_{-1},\qquad H:(1,2)_{1/2}."""),
        ("p", r"Two further generations repeat the pattern with larger masses. The electric charge is $Q=T_3+Y$, with $T_3=\pm\frac12$ the weak isospin of a doublet member. This gives $2/3$ and $-1/3$ for the quarks and $0$ and $-1$ for the leptons. The hypercharges are tightly constrained. Counting colours and writing right-handed fields as left-handed antiparticles, the sums of $Y$ and of $Y^3$ over one generation both vanish. These cancellations of gauge anomalies, required for a consistent quantum theory, work only between quarks and leptons together."),
        ("p", r"The Higgs doublet $H$ has a vacuum expectation value $v/\sqrt2$ in its neutral component. Its kinetic energy then gives mass to three combinations of the gauge fields, the $W^\pm$ and the $Z$, and leaves the photon massless. With $g$ and $g'$ the $SU(2)_L$ and $U(1)_Y$ couplings and $\theta_W$ the weak mixing angle,"),
        ("eq", r"""m_W=\frac{gv}{2},\qquad m_Z=\frac{\sqrt{g^2+g'^2}\,v}{2},\qquad
\tan\theta_W=\frac{g'}{g},\\
e=g\sin\theta_W,\qquad m_W=m_Z\cos\theta_W."""),
        ("p", r"Muon decay measures the Fermi constant $G_F$, and W exchange gives $G_F/\sqrt2=g^2/8m_W^2$. Hence $v=(\sqrt2G_F)^{-1/2}=246.2$ GeV. Eliminating $g$ with $e^2=4\pi\alpha$ gives a prediction for the W mass:"),
        ("eq", r"""m_W^2=\frac{\pi\alpha}{\sqrt2\,G_F\sin^2\theta_W},\qquad
m_Z=\frac{m_W}{\cos\theta_W}."""),
        ("p", r"Take $G_F=1.1664\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{GeV^{-2}}$, $\sin^2\theta_W=0.231$ from Z-pole asymmetries, and the coupling at the Z mass, $\alpha\approx1/128$. The formula then gives $m_W\approx80.3$ GeV and $m_Z\approx91.5$ GeV, both within half a percent of the measured values. With the low-energy value $\alpha=1/137$ the same formula gives 77.5 GeV, so the running described under Renormalization is required. The remaining difference is a loop correction dominated by the top quark and proportional to $G_Fm_t^2$. Its size indicated the top-quark mass before the quark was observed in 1995."),
        ("p", r"With 19 parameters, the model accounts for collider measurements of electroweak and strong processes. Its tree-level relations hold at the percent level, and tests at the per-mille level require loop corrections, so they test the quantum theory itself. Neutrino oscillations show that neutrinos have mass, which the minimal model with only left-handed neutrinos lacks. Gravity, dark matter and the excess of matter over antimatter also lie outside it."),
    ],
}

